  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Crepidotus variabilis, known as the Variable Oysterling, is a small wood-decay fungus characterized by its fan or oyster-shaped caps and cream to pale brown coloring. This delicate species belongs to the Crepidotaceae family and is common throughout New England's forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Shape",
      "description": "Fan or oyster-shaped caps, typically 0.5-2 cm wide, with a whitish to pale tan surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Arrangement",
      "description": "Gills radiate from a central attachment point and are pale to cream-colored, becoming tan with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "No Stem",
      "description": "Mushroom is entirely stemless or nearly so, attaching directly to wood at the side or base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Variable Oysterlings grow on decaying hardwood logs, branches, and woody debris in moist forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its small size, Crepidotus variabilis plays an important ecological role as a wood decomposer, helping break down fallen logs and returning nutrients to the forest soil.",
